    },
    {
     "kind": "read",
     "step": "read",
     "title": "Definite integral by anti-derivatives",
     "paras": [
      "If F is any anti-derivative of f: ∫ from a to b of f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a).",
      "a and b are the limits of integration.",
      "Notation: [F(x)] from a to b = F(b) − F(a)."
     ],
     "note": "Fundamental Theorem: the area function A(x) has A′(x) = f(x).",
     "ref": "Definition 2.15, Theorem 2.7",
     "id": "g12.maths.u2.s2-3-3.2"
    },
    {
     "kind": "read",
     "step": "read",
     "title": "Properties",
     "paras": [
      "∫ from a to a = 0.",
      "∫ from a to b = ∫ from a to c + ∫ from c to b.",
      "∫ from a to b = −∫ from b to a.",
      "∫ kf = k ∫ f, and ∫ (f ± g) = ∫ f ± ∫ g (same limits)."
